import pf.Html
import ../../Demos/Birds
import ../../Demos/Snake
import ../../Demos/Timers
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Callout
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/Feature
import ../../Ui/FeatureGrid
import ../../Ui/Note
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/Demo

meta = {
	title: "Showcase",
	description: "A game of Snake, a flock of bouncing Roc birds and the browser's timers: components that Roc, compiled to WebAssembly, runs frame by frame in your browser, with sound from a handler.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "snake", label: "Snake" },
		{ id: "flock", label: "A flock of Roc birds" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How they work" },
		{ id: "sound", label: "Sound, from a handler" },
		{ id: "timers", label: "Timers" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="Showcase"
				title="A game and a flock, run by Roc"
			>
				These are components like any other: the server rendered them with this page, and your browser
				hydrated them. What is different is that they run on time, not only on clicks. Each animation
				frame calls a handler in Roc, which moves things. Then only the holes that read what moved run
				again, and they patch the few attributes that changed. Below the demos: how they work, how a
				handler plays a sound, and the browser's timers.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="snake">Snake</Heading>
			<p>
				Click the board, then steer with the arrow keys or WASD. Space pauses and M mutes. The arrows
				don't scroll the page while you play, and Tab still takes you out of the game. Pick a level:
				Easy, Medium and Hard each keep one speed, and on Impossible every fruit makes the snake faster.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo game meter="snake" idle="Roc is idle until you start: no frames run while the game waits.">
			<div data-demo="snake"><Snake seed={7} /></div>
		</Demo>

		<NoHydration>
			<Heading id="flock">A flock of Roc birds</Heading>
			<p>
				Say how many birds you want, by typing a number or with −5, −1, +1 and +5: new ones come in, or
				the newest fly away. Each flies in its own direction, bounces off the walls and off the others,
				and squeaks when it bumps into one (once you've clicked something on the page: browsers keep
				pages quiet until then).
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo game meter="birds" idle="Measuring…">
			<div data-demo="birds"><Birds count={4} /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<Note>
				The line under each demo is measured here, as it runs. A frame's time covers the handler that
				moves every bird and settles every pair that meets, then the holes that read the flock (each
				bird's position among them), and the patches they make.
			</Note>

			<Heading id="how">How they work</Heading>
			<FeatureGrid>
				<Feature title="Time is a handler">
					<p>
						<code>on:frame.ms={"{|ms| send!(Frame(ms))}"}</code> runs its handler every animation frame,
						given the milliseconds since the last, for as long as its element is on the page. The snake
						puts it on an element inside an <code>if</code> that holds only while you play, so a paused
						game runs no frames at all. An empty sky has no such element either.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Keys that do one thing">
					<p>
						<code>on:keydown.ArrowUp={"{|| send!(Turn(Up))}"}</code> runs for that key only, and stops it
						from scrolling the page. Keys no handler names, Tab among them, do what they always do.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Rows that stay, so little changes">
					<p>
						The birds are a <code>.map</code>'s rows, keyed by id, so each bird keeps its element, and a
						move only sets two custom properties, which the stylesheet turns into a translation. The
						snake's segments are rows by position (<code>{".map_with_index(|part, i| … key={i})"}</code>):
						when it moves, each takes the cell of the one ahead, and a CSS transition as long as a step
						slides it there.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Pure, so it replays">
					<p>
						The rules are a plain function, <code>update</code>, which makes the next game from the last
						one and what you did; the component keeps the game in a signal. Roc has no hidden
						randomness: the food's next place and a new bird's heading come from a seed kept in the game
						(<code>Demos/Random.roc</code>). The same moves give the same game, and every rule is an
						<code>expect</code> away from a test.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="What isn't shown isn't read">
					<p>
						The snake moves one cell every step's worth of frames. The time since its last move is a
						signal of its own, which nothing on the page reads, so a frame that only adds to it runs no
						hole and patches nothing.
					</p>
				</Feature>
			</FeatureGrid>
			<details>
				<summary>The snake: <code>{Sources.demo_snake.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_snake.file} source={Sources.demo_snake.rocx} />
			</details>
			<details>
				<summary>The flock: <code>{Sources.demo_birds.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_birds.file} source={Sources.demo_birds.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="sound">Sound, from a handler</Heading>
			<p>
				The game's rules are pure: <code>update</code> can't make a sound, only the next game. So the
				next game says what its step sounded like (<code>"eat"</code>, <code>"die"</code>, or nothing),
				which a test can check, and the handler that sets the game's signal plays it with
				<code>Audio.play!</code>, from the <a href={Paths.ecosystem_audio}>audio</a> package (a plugin,
				whose script in the page does the playing):
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.demo_snake.file} source={"go! = |next| {\n\tset_game!(next)\n\tif (!next.muted and !next.sound.is_empty()) Audio.play!(\"/sounds/\${next.sound}.wav\") else {}\n}"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				It plays any file the browser can (WAV, MP3 or Ogg, from the site or anywhere else) with nothing
				prepared in advance: the browser fetches a file the first time, and keeps it. These are a few KB
				each, chiptune notes made by a script (<code>site/sounds.mjs</code>). The flock plays one pop at
				every pitch, with <code>{"Audio.play_with!(url, { volume, rate })"}</code>: each bird more is a
				higher note, the same file played a little faster.
			</p>
			<p>
				A sound plays once the handler is done and the page is patched with what it wrote. Only a
				handler can play one: called while a hole is computed, or on the server, it fails. For anything
				else the browser can do that the platform has no module for (the clipboard, say), a handler calls
				the page's own JavaScript with <code>{"Browser.call!(name, arg)"}</code>, as Elm's ports do. It
				gets no answer back: for that, ask the server (<a href={Paths.calls}>talking to the server</a>).
			</p>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Watch it happen">
				<p>
					Open your browser's element inspector on a bird while it flies: its <code>style</code> is the
					only thing that changes, frame after frame, and only in the hundredths Roc rounds it to.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="timers">Timers</Heading>
			<p>
				A frame is the clock for motion. For “every tenth of a second” and “once the typing stops”, a
				component has the browser's timers, from <code>pf.Solid</code>.
				<code>set_interval!(ms, tick!)</code> and <code>set_timeout!(ms, done!)</code> run a handler
				later, as a click runs one, and return a <code>clear!</code> function that stops it.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo game>
			<div data-demo="timers"><Timers /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<p>
				The stopwatch keeps its interval's <code>clear!</code> in a signal, as JavaScript keeps an
				interval's id, and Stop calls it:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.demo_timers.file} source={"(watch!, set_watch!) = create_signal!(stopped)\ntoggle! = ||\n\tmatch watch!() {\n\t\tRunning(clear!) => {\n\t\t\tclear!()\n\t\t\tset_watch!(Stopped)\n\t\t}\n\t\tStopped => set_watch!(Running(set_interval!(100, || set_tenths!(tenths!() + 1))))\n\t}"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The search clears the last key's timeout at each key and starts its own, so it searches once,
				400 ms after the last key. The clock clears nothing. In Solid, a component stops its own timer
				with <code>{"onCleanup(() => clearInterval(id))"}</code>; here that is built in. A timer belongs
				to what started it: a component's timer to the component, a handler's to the part of the page its
				element is in. When that goes (as the clock does when the <code>if</code> around it hides it),
				its timers stop, and the ticks you hear stop with them.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="note" title="The server starts none">
				<p>
					While the server renders the page, no timer runs: the clock is rendered at 0, and the
					browser starts its interval when it hydrates the page.
				</p>
			</Callout>
			<details>
				<summary>The timers, and the clock: <code>{Sources.demo_timers.file}</code>, <code>{Sources.demo_clock.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_timers.file} source={Sources.demo_timers.rocx} />
				<Code title={Sources.demo_clock.file} source={Sources.demo_clock.rocx} />
			</details>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
